WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.320
Two structural features decide whether a fatty acid is solid or liquid.

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.120
First, chain length.

3
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:14.000
Among saturated fatty acids, longer chains pack more tightly and melt higher.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:20.520
Lauric acid at 12 carbons melts near 44 degrees, palmitic at 16 near 63, stearic at

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.280
18 near 70. Second, saturation.

6
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.060
One cis double bond kinks the chain and breaks the packing.

7
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.200
Stearic acid is a solid.

8
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.260
Oleic acid with a single double bond melts at just 13 degrees and pours as an oil.

9
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.820
More double bonds drop it further still.

10
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.280
This is the whole chapter in one graph.

11
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.940
A triacylglycerol's melting point depends mostly on which fatty acids hang off the

12
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.720
glycerol. Tripalmitin, three saturated 16-carbon chains, packs tightly and is solid

13
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.720
at body temperature.

14
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.040
Triolein, three unsaturated chains, with 18 carbons and one double bond, stays

15
00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.560
liquid even at room temperature.

16
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.500
A mixed triacylglycerol, one saturated chain and two unsaturated, can melt well

17
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.660
below body temperature.

18
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.820
Natural fats are mixtures of many such molecules.

19
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.640
Cells fine-tune the physical state of their fat by choosing which fatty acid goes to

20
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.000
which position on glycerol.

21
00:01:17.983 --> 00:01:23.143
Fatty acid saturation is the main dial a cell turns to tune membrane fluidity.

22
00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:28.543
Saturated tails pack tightly through van der Waals contacts, raising the transition

23
00:01:28.543 --> 00:01:31.003
temperature and pushing the membrane toward a gel.

24
00:01:31.823 --> 00:01:36.543
Unsaturated tails kink and create packing defects, keeping the membrane fluid even

25
00:01:36.543 --> 00:01:42.243
when it is cold. The E. coli example is elegant: grown colder, the bacterium makes

26
00:01:42.243 --> 00:01:45.463
more unsaturated fatty acids to hold its fluidity steady.

27
00:01:45.463 --> 00:01:51.363
Tuning composition to keep viscosity constant as the environment changes is

28
00:01:51.363 --> 00:01:52.783
homeoviscous adaptation.

29
00:01:54.017 --> 00:01:56.837
Cholesterol is a fluidity buffer, a thermostat.

30
00:01:57.357 --> 00:02:01.137
At low temperature, it inserts between the tails and disrupts tight packing,

31
00:02:01.457 --> 00:02:02.857
preventing the membrane from gelling.

32
00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:07.557
At high temperature, it restrains the same tails, keeping the membrane from becoming

33
00:02:07.557 --> 00:02:12.217
too fluid. On a graph of fluidity against temperature, cholesterol flattens the

34
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:16.657
sharp phase transition into a broad, gentle curve, giving the cell a stable

35
00:02:16.657 --> 00:02:18.957
operating range across a wide window.

36
00:02:19.617 --> 00:02:21.617
One molecule, two opposite jobs.
